 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS. The server commits each accepted edit with one event revision and pushes committed revisions to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets. A browser that reconnects requests the revisions it missed and resumes from the last revision it applied.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The project runs its own connection capacity and reconnect handling. This trades operational work for fast updates without a recurring realtime vendor fee and without coupling the board to one provider's subscription model and credentials. Subsecond updates are not required; the freshness target is 15 seconds under normal connectivity.
+
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around that approach. Change storage keeps an ordered revision log that delivery can replay. Delivery publishes each committed revision to the sockets it manages. Browser sync resumes from the revision it last applied.
 
